  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 936 AM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nThe entire region is under a Flood Watch from 12:00 PM MDT this \nafternoon until 7:00 PM MDT Wednesday evening. Flash flooding is\nlikely with thunderstorms for all parts of the region during this\ntime.\n\nAn upper-level high over the southern Plains and a trough moving \nthrough California and Nevada will facilitate a tight plume of \nmonsoonal moisture from the Gulf of California into our region \nstarting this afternoon. The backdoor cold front that moved into\nour region from the Texas Panhandle and eastern New Mexico last\nnight will assist in moisture convergence at the surface. All of\nthis will result in several rounds of heavy thunderstorms through\nWednesday evening. The overnight hours tonight into Wednesday \nmorning is expected to be the most critical period for heavy \nrainfall potential.\n\nAs it stands, all models are favoring the heaviest precipitation\nthrough Wednesday evening to be centralized in northern Dona Ana\nCounty and western Sierra County, NM (especially in the Black\nRange). The most likely rainfall scenario would see isolated \npockets of 3 to 5 inches within the broader 2-3 inch area through \nThursday. A fair amount of the heavier rain will likely fall into \nbasins that feed in south of Caballo Dam (everything south of \nPercha Creek), which will likely bring significant rises in the \nRio Grande tonight and Wednesday.\n\nFlash flooding will continue to be a hazard on Thursday, though\nrainfall totals will be slightly diminished compared to today and\nWednesday, particularly out west. Flooding will be more of a risk\nout east in the Sacramento Mountains and the Texas Rio Grande\nValley. As Thursday turns to Friday, rain will shift further east\nas an upper-level low moves over the Four Corners and renders\nshowers and storms as widely scattered.\n\nQuieter conditions will prevail this weekend as a ridge builds\nover the lower Rockies, but an active tropical Pacific and new\nWest Coast trough could bring moisture back to the region early\nnext week. It remains to be seen how much rain the region will\nget.",
